course: everyday
lesson: 43
title: Buses, trains and being seen
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Getting on and off buses and trains is a time when children are close to big vehicles whose drivers cannot see everything, so you wait well back until the vehicle has stopped.
explain: In the USA and Canada, a stopped school bus with flashing red lights and its stop sign arm out means traffic must stop; you cross in front of the bus, several big steps ahead where the driver can see you, and never behind it.
explain: On a train platform you stand behind the yellow or white line, let people off before you get on, and mind the gap between the train and the platform.
explain: At night a driver sees a child in dark clothes very late; reflective strips shine the headlights straight back to the driver, so a boy in Alaska walking home in winter darkness with a reflective band can be seen from far away.
explain: In daylight, bright fluorescent colours help you stand out; at night it is reflective material and lights that work.
explain: A boy in Texas ran around the front of his school bus to cross the road; a car passing the bus did not stop, and it hit him.
explain: A girl in Scotland walked along a country road at night in dark clothes; a driver saw her only at the last moment and nearly hit her.
fact: You wait for a bus well back from the edge of the road until it has fully stopped.
fact: The area all around a school bus where the driver cannot see you well is called the danger zone.
fact: You cross in front of a school bus, far enough ahead that the driver can see you, never behind it.
fact: Before you cross in front of a school bus, you look at the driver and wait for a wave or nod that it is safe.
fact: In the USA and Canada, traffic must stop for a school bus with flashing red lights and its stop arm out.
fact: On a moving bus you stay seated, or hold on tight if you must stand.
fact: On a train platform you stand behind the yellow or white safety line.
fact: A fast train rushing past pushes and pulls the air, which can drag someone standing too close to the edge.
fact: You let passengers get off a train or bus before you get on.
fact: Reflective material sends a car's headlight beam back towards the driver.
fact: Fluorescent colours show up best in daylight and do little at night.
fact: Where there is no pavement, you walk facing the oncoming traffic, so you can see it coming.
fact: A bike ridden at night needs a white light at the front and a red light at the back.
fact: If you are separated from your adult on a train or at a station, you tell a member of staff.
fact: Drivers see someone in dark clothes at night much later than someone wearing bright or reflective things.
fact: A train can take more than a mile to stop, so it cannot stop for someone on the track; you cross railway lines only by a bridge or a proper crossing.
exercise: When should you step towards a bus that is pulling up at your stop? | When it has stopped completely and the door opens | As it slows down, so you are first on and get a good seat | While it is still moving slowly | As soon as you can see its number
exercise: A boy in Ohio gets off the school bus and must cross the road. Where should he cross? | In front of the bus, several steps ahead, where the driver sees him | Right behind the bus, where it is shady | Close against the front of the bus | Wherever he likes, as all the traffic in both directions has stopped
exercise: Why should you never cross behind a school bus? | The driver cannot see you there, and passing cars cannot either | The exhaust pipe is very hot | Buses often reverse at every stop to line up with the kerb and pavement | Behind the bus is always muddy
exercise: What does a school bus with flashing red lights and its stop arm out tell drivers in the USA? | They must stop and not pass the bus | They should slow down and pass carefully | They may pass if they honk | Only cars behind the bus need to stop
exercise: What is the danger zone around a school bus? | The area close to the bus where the driver cannot see you well | The road far ahead of the bus, past where it will pull in and stop | The seats at the back of the bus | The bus stop shelter
exercise: You drop a pencil next to the wheel of the school bus. What should you do? | Tell the driver, and do not reach under the bus | Quickly crawl under to grab it | Kick it out with your foot | Wait until the bus starts to move away, then dart in and pick it up
exercise: Where should you stand while waiting on a train platform? | Behind the yellow or white safety line | Right at the edge, ready to board | On the line, so the driver sees you | Wherever there is space, even at the edge
exercise: Why do you let passengers off the train before you get on? | So people can leave without crowding and you board safely | Because the train will not move until you get on | So that you get the best seat | Because there is a law about which door each person must use to get on
exercise: A girl in London gets on the train but the doors close before her mum gets on. What is a sensible plan? | Get off at the next station and tell a member of staff | Pull the doors open with her hands | Stay on alone until the very end of the line and wait there | Get off and walk back along the tracks
exercise: Why does a reflective strip help at night? | It sends the headlight beam back to the driver | It glows all by itself in the dark like a torch | It makes a sound when a car comes | It heats up so cars can sense it
exercise: Why does a fluorescent yellow jacket do little at night? | It needs daylight to glow brightly | It is too thin to be seen | Its colour fades in the cold | Drivers cannot see yellow at all
exercise: Which clothing makes you hardest for a driver to see at night? | Dark clothes with no reflectors | A white top with a reflective band | A jacket with reflective stripes | Carrying a small torch
exercise: There is no pavement on a country road. Which side should you walk on? | The side where you face the oncoming traffic | The side where traffic comes from behind you | Right in the middle of the road | On whichever side is smoothest
exercise: What lights does a bike need when it is ridden at night? | White at the front and red at the back | Red at the front and white at the back | Only one bright light at the front | Flashing blue lights on both ends
test: A girl in Toronto is running late, and the school bus is already pulling away from her stop. What should she do? | Let it go and not chase it into the road | Run after it and bang on the door until it stops | Run in front of it to make it stop | Grab the back of the bus
test: A driver in Texas sees a school bus stopped ahead with red lights flashing and the stop arm out. What must he do? | Stop and wait until the lights stop flashing | Pass it slowly on the left | Honk so the children hurry | Pass quickly before more children get out of the bus
test: A boy in Brisbane walks home from a friend's house after dark along a road with no footpath. What helps most? | Walking facing the traffic, wearing something reflective | Walking with his back to the traffic, in dark clothes | Walking in the middle of the road | Running so that he is home sooner
test: Which is true about being seen? | Fluorescent colours help by day, reflective strips at night | Fluorescent colours help most at night | Reflective strips work only in sunshine | Dark clothes are fine at night, as long as the street lamps are on
test: Why should a child step back from the platform edge when a fast train is passing? | The rush of air and the train itself can pull or hit someone close | Trains spray water when they pass | Fast trains are always about to stop at the platform, so you must make room for them | The platform edge is only for staff
test: A boy has to stand on a busy bus with no free seats. What should he do? | Hold a handrail firmly | Stand in the middle without holding on | Lean against the doors | Sit on the floor near the driver
test: Which is the safest way to get off a school bus and cross the street? | Walk ahead of the bus, look at the driver, wait for a signal, then look both ways | Run straight out from the back of the bus | Cross right next to the front bumper, close enough to touch it, so the driver knows you are there | Walk under the stop arm while it is moving
test: Why should a cyclist riding at dusk, as it gets dark, have a red light at the back? | So drivers coming up behind can see the bike | So the cyclist can see the road behind in the dark | So other cyclists know to stop | So the bike looks like a car
test: Two friends get separated from their teacher in a busy station. What is the best thing to do? | Tell a member of staff and wait with them | Leave the station to look for the teacher | Get on any train that comes | Hide until the teacher finds them
test: school bus stopped, cross behind it? | No, cross in front where the driver can see you | Yes, behind is safest, cars stop for buses anyway | Yes, run behind quickly | Crawl under it
test: bus coming, stand right at the edge? | No, wait well back till it stops | Yes, so the driver sees you and stops | Yes, lean out to wave | Yes, on the road itself
test: train platform, can i stand over the yellow line | No, stay behind it | Yes, to get on first | Yes, if no train is coming | Yes, if you hold on
test: walking home at night, fluoro or reflective | Reflective, it shines in headlights | Fluorescent works best at night, it glows | Dark clothes are fine | Neither matters
test: walking home in the dark, all black clothes, it's fine right | Wear something bright or reflective so drivers see you | Black is fine, cars have lights so they'll see you anyway | Walk faster so it is shorter | Walk in the middle of the road
test: mates are taking a shortcut over the train tracks | Say no and use the bridge or crossing | Go if you cannot see or hear a train coming | Go if you run | Go if you listen first
test: dropped my pencil case under the bus as it's leaving | Leave it and tell the driver or an adult | Crawl under to grab it | Run to the front to get it | Pick it up from behind the back wheel before it rolls
test: did u hear a kid ran behind the school bus and a car nearly got him cos it couldn't see him | Real lesson: never cross behind a bus; cross in front where the driver can see you | Real lesson: school buses should never stop on busy roads where cars go past | It proves only the bus driver can cause accidents | It is gossip, so crossing behind is fine
test: my brother reckons bright neon clothes make u glow at night so drivers see u | Not true; fluorescent works by day, but at night you need reflective stuff | He is right, neon glows in the dark | He is right, if the clothes are new | He is right, neon and reflective are the same thing, they both light up in the dark
test: Max fell over on the train when it moved and everyone laughed, gonna post it lol | Do not post it; it would embarrass him, and anyone can lose balance on a train | Post it, because falling on trains is funny and everyone already saw it happen anyway | Post it, so he holds on next time | Post it, but put a funny caption
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the danger zone surrounding a school bus? | The area about the bus within which the driver cannot see a child well | The area within the bus reserved for teachers | The road ahead of the bus where it may not stop | The bus stop at which the bus waits whilst the children board and alight in turn
exam: It being given that a school bus in the United States displays flashing red lights and its stop arm, determine the obligation of other traffic. | Other traffic must stop | Other traffic may pass at reduced speed | Other traffic must sound the horn | Other traffic may continue if no child is visible
exam: Identify the side of a road without pavement upon which a pedestrian ought to walk. | Facing the oncoming traffic, so that it may be seen approaching | With the traffic, so that it approaches from behind and may pass safely | In the middle of the road for visibility | On whichever side has more shade
exam: Select the response that is consistent with established guidance at a train platform. | Persons should stand behind the yellow or white safety line | Persons should stand at the very edge of the platform | Persons should board before others alight | Persons should run towards the train as it arrives
